---
/**
 * The days before: an archive of the last fortnight of valleys.
 *
 * The page is a shell on purpose. Every date on it is relative to the person
 * reading it, and the site's day boundary is their own midnight, so the list
 * can only be worked out in the browser: see the package's PastDays. What is
 * here is the chrome, the copy and the promise that nothing was stored.
 */
import PastDays from '@codemanethan/genesis/PastDays';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="The days before · Ethan Plunk"
  description="Two weeks of valleys, none of them saved. Every day on this site is generated from its own date, so an old day can be built again from nothing but the date it was: towns, founders, weather and all."
  image="/screenshots/genesis.png"
>
  <section class="intro">
    <nav class="local" aria-label="Genesis">
      <a href="/genesis/">The valley</a>
      <a href="/projects/genesis/">The project</a>
    </nav>
    <p class="kicker">The archive that isn&rsquo;t one</p>
    <h1>The days before</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The valley is thrown away every midnight, and none of them are kept anywhere &mdash; not on
      a server, not in this page. They don&rsquo;t need to be. A day <em>is</em> its date: the
      date is hashed into a seed, and the seed is the whole world. Give it back and the valley
      comes back with it, the same river, the same towns, the same names, down to the cart on the
      bridge.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      So here is the last fortnight, rebuilt in your browser while you look at it. Each picture is
      the real world, held at half past eight in the evening; open one and you land on that same
      hour. One thing does carry between days &mdash; every valley inherits a ruin from the day
      before it, yesterday&rsquo;s landmark left standing in today&rsquo;s fields. It is the only
      thread there is.
    </p>
    <div class="intro-actions">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/genesis/">The valley &rarr;</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/projects/genesis/">How it works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="days" aria-label="The last fourteen days">
    <PastDays client:only="react" />
    <noscript>
      <p class="noscript">
        The days are generated in the browser, so this list needs JavaScript. Nothing is lost if
        you&rsquo;d rather not: <a href="/projects/genesis/">the Genesis project page</a> describes
        exactly how a date becomes a valley, and <a href="/#projects">the project list</a> has
        every project in plain form.
      </p>
    </noscript>
  </section>

  <p class="back-row"><a class="back" href="/genesis/">&larr; The valley</a></p>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro {
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.6rem;
  }

  .local {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .local a {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .local a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .kicker {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--teal);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .intro-actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
  }

  /* The day cards are the package's markup and read these names with the
     package's own fallbacks; scoped here so the white cards keep the
     package's palette instead of picking up the site's dark-page tokens. */
  .days {
    --card: #ffffff;
    --border: #ece3d2;
    --ink-soft: #7a7690;
    --mint: #63c9a8;
    color: #413a55;
  }

  .noscript {
    max-width: 46rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .back-row {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
